{
  "id": "u1-l5",
  "unitId": "unit-1",
  "order": 5,
  "title": "One key, two names",
  "summary": "C♯ and D♭ are the same key. Notes like these are called enharmonic.",
  "minutes": 5,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Enharmonic names",
      "body": "You may have noticed that every black key got two names. C♯ and D♭ are the same key and the same sound. Names like these are **enharmonic**.\n\nBoth names are correct. Which one musicians write depends on the key of the song, which comes in a later unit. For now, know both.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        61
      ],
      "labels": {
        "61": "C♯ / D♭"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "show",
      "title": "Both names of each black key",
      "body": "- C♯ = D♭\n- D♯ = E♭\n- F♯ = G♭\n- G♯ = A♭\n- A♯ = B♭",
      "highlightMidi": [
        61,
        63,
        66,
        68,
        70
      ],
      "labels": {
        "61": "C♯ / D♭",
        "63": "D♯ / E♭",
        "66": "F♯ / G♭",
        "68": "G♯ / A♭",
        "70": "A♯ / B♭"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "White keys can do it too",
      "body": "There is no black key between E and F, or between B and C. So **E♯** is just F, and **C♭** is just B. You won't see these often, but now they won't surprise you when you do.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        64,
        65
      ],
      "labels": {
        "64": "E",
        "65": "F = E♯"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "show",
      "title": "E and F, B and C",
      "body": "The two places where white keys sit side by side with no black key between them.",
      "highlightMidi": [
        64,
        65,
        71,
        72
      ],
      "labels": {
        "64": "E",
        "65": "F",
        "71": "B = C♭",
        "72": "C"
      }
    },
